[[compiler-realization]]
=== Realization

[abstract]
A Morloc term may have several implementations: sourced functions in
different languages, several in one language, and definitions written in
Morloc itself. Realization chooses one implementation for every use of a term
and one language for every node of each exported tree. It sits between
typechecking and segmentation: its input is a typed tree in which each use of a
term still carries all of its alternatives, and its output is a tree in which
each node has exactly one implementation and one language, or no language at
all when the nexus evaluates it. This section specifies the search space, the
score being minimized, how ties are broken, and the placement rules that
the score alone does not decide.

**What is chosen.** The user-facing view is in <<term-polymorphism>>: one
name, many definitions. The author's claim in declaring them under one name is
that they compute the same value, so realization treats them as
interchangeable and chooses among them on cost alone. A term defined in Morloc
has no language of its own. Its body is realized wherever its uses need it, so
one definition can run in Python at one call and in {cpp} at another. A
shared value or specialization (<<compiler-sharing>>) is scored once and then
realized once for each language its uses land in, each copy under its own
name. The result is the same every time the same program is built by the same
compiler.

**Search.** Realization is a two-pass dynamic program over each tree.

* *Scoring*, bottom up, gives every node a table from language to the best
  score of its subtree when that node runs in that language. A sourced call
  has one entry, its own language. An application combines its head's table
  with, for each argument, the cheapest way to have that argument in the
  head's language. A node with several alternatives takes, per language, the
  cheapest alternative. Literals, containers and other language-free nodes
  take the languages their context offers.
* *Collapse*, top down, gives the root its cheapest language and then gives
  each child the language that minimizes its own score plus the cost of the
  edge from its parent's language. At a node with alternatives, the cheapest
  alternative from the parent's language is kept and the rest are discarded.

Before scoring, applications of a literal lambda whose parameter is used at
most once are reduced, so that a long point-free composition does not make
the scorer revisit the same function arguments again and again.

**Cost.** A score counts crossings between pools, grouped by depth,
then changes of language within one pool, then the sourced calls placed in
each language, grouped by the language's rank in a fixed preference order,
then the number of sourced calls. Scores are added along the tree and
compared part by part in that order: crossings from the greatest depth
down, so one crossing at a greater depth outweighs any number at a smaller
one;
calls from the least preferred language upward.

[cols="1,4", options="header"]
|===
| Part | Meaning
| Crossing | An edge between two languages in different pools is one crossing, whatever values cross it. A value made in one pool, passed through a second and used in a third crosses twice. An edge between languages that share a pool, such as a guest language in its host's pool (<<compiler-onboarding>>), is a switch.
| Depth | Code at the root of an exported function is at depth 0. A function or suspension passed to a sourced function, or to a shared specialization, is one deeper than the call, and the body of a recursive definition is one deeper than the call that enters it: either may run more than once per call. A function value passed to a definition in Morloc is counted where the body uses it.
| Preference | The order is one table shipped with the compiler: compiled languages first, then interpreted ones, a language with no entry last. It decides only between placements that cross equally often at every depth.
| Size | Fewer sourced calls wins, so a sourced implementation beats a Morloc definition that does the same work in the same languages by calling several.
|===

Code the nexus evaluates costs nothing, and starting a pool costs nothing,
since pools start once per run.

**Ties.** Before cost, alternatives whose types differ are separated by
type: the most specific instance whose type matches the use wins, and an
instance on a nearer alias beats one on a farther alias. Two instances that
both match with neither more specific are rejected as ambiguous. A node
with no implementation of its own, such as a `let` or a container, keeps its
parent's language when its options tie. Two sources of one term in one
language are refused before realization; two sources still tied after every
part of the score are refused at build, naming both. Definitions in Morloc
that tie are interchangeable, and a fixed one is used, so the choice never
varies between builds.

**Checking the built placement.** Collapse is meant to build the placement
scoring found cheapest. With verbosity, `morloc typecheck --realize` scores
the built tree again with every node fixed to the language and
implementation collapse chose, and lists any difference beside the
placement. A node built in a language its table does not offer, such as a
lambda built in its parent's language around a body placed elsewhere, is
priced as reaching its table from there.

**Placement rules.** Some placements are fixed rather than scored:

* A sourced function runs in the language it is sourced from.
* A lambda is built in its parent's language; only its body chooses, and a
  call the body makes elsewhere is an ordinary crossing there.
* A function value stored in a container, or passed directly to a sourced
  function, is homed in that container's or host's language, so the host
  receives a native callable (<<compiler-calls>>).
* A value bound by a `let` that holds a function and computes nothing to
  build, such as a lambda or a record of lambdas and suspensions, is built
  again in each language that uses it, so a use in another language calls
  its own copy rather than crossing back to the first. A bound function
  that is computed is built once, in its definition's best language, and
  every use reaches it there.
* A function that sourced code makes stays in its pool. The compiler
  follows where every function value was made through every construct and
  every call: a sourced call makes each function in its result, unless the
  source's signature gives that position a type variable that one of its
  parameters also holds, in which case the result holds what the arguments
  held there; a function a source passes to a callback is made by that
  source; a lambda, a partial application or a suspension that Morloc
  builds is Morloc's. A function inside a record field, a list element or
  a value of an alias type is followed like any other, and a function
  passed to a source returns its result where that source calls it, so a
  function another pool made cannot come back out of it. Every expression
  that holds or receives a function
  sourced code made, and the expression consuming it, is placed in that
  source's language, and an argument computed from one is computed there so
  only its value crosses. A recursive function given one is built again for
  that language. A program that would carry one into another language, or
  serialize one, is refused at build.
* When the call that makes such a function has sources in several
  languages, the source is chosen for the call alone; to have another make
  it, the program imports only the module that sources it in the language
  needed. A call given such a function may instead use its term's source in
  the function's language, when the term has one with the same call groups.
* A language-free operation (building a list, tuple or record, applying a
  function value, a bracket access) runs in its context's language only if
  that language can represent every type it works on. Otherwise it moves to
  the cheapest language that can.
* A conversion between a newtype and its representation runs in a language
  where the newtype has no native form of its own, since only there is the
  conversion free.
* Every node on a recursive cycle, from the recursive term down to each call
  that refers back to it, is placed in that term's language or in a
  language that shares its pool. A cycle that crossed pools would nest one
  foreign call per level and park a worker in each pool per level, so its
  depth would be bounded by the number of workers rather than by memory.
  Kept in one pool, the cycle can become a loop (<<compiler-calls>>), and the
  foreign calls its body makes are a bounded number per step. The outermost
  recursive term wins, so nested cycles agree.

**Nexus or pool.** A tree that calls nothing sourced gets no language. The
nexus evaluates it directly, and the manifest marks its command `pure`
(<<build-artifacts>>). The nexus evaluator has no function values and cannot
call a function by name, so two cases still need a pool. A term that calls
itself is given one of the program's pool languages, chosen by the same
scoring; if the program imports no language at all, the build fails and
asks for one. A shared specialization that takes a function argument is
expanded at each of its nexus uses, while a first-order one is called by
name. A language-free subtree used inside a realized tree runs in the pool of
its context, exactly as if it had been written there.

**Result.** Every node of a realized tree carries one implementation and one
language. The set of languages that appear is the set of pools the program
needs, and every edge between two languages that do not share a pool is a
cross-language call. Segmentation cuts the tree at exactly those edges
(<<compiler-calls>>).

